#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""task-agent: the agent that works tasks handed to it at http://beacon.

Every minute a systemd timer on the agent's home machine runs this once (linux-setup `apps/task-agent`); systemd
never starts a second run while one is going, so one task at a time:

  -. loose ends first, quietly: a task a dead run left `doing` is given back, and a PR merged
     or closed on GitHub moves its task (done, or back to him), checked every 10 minutes;
  0. route every new task (filed since ROUTE_SINCE and 5 minutes old, mostly by Claude sessions): one cheap
     tool-less model call says agent (it gets the `claude` tag), his (a note says why), or ask
     (one question for him on Telegram). The Hand to agent button still works by hand;
  1. take the most urgent open `todo` task tagged `claude` (parked ones wait, and so does one
     whose body says `Not before YYYY-MM-DD` until that day; at most MAX_RUNS_A_DAY runs in 24 h);
  2. claim it: status `doing`, a note, and the session id it is about to run under, linked, so
     the task page shows the agent live (state and what it is doing, from /api/live);
  3. gather what he typed in the sessions linked to it (the one that filed it) from the hub, which
     has every machine's prompts, since the agent can't read a session on another machine;
  4. run headless Claude Code on it with the ship-task skill in a fresh git worktree of the
     task's repo (never a checkout, which may be a live app), unattended: a task with no
     Done-test gets one written first (or a question), doc-sync and a `low` code review come
     before any PR, and it stops at an open PR noted on the task, closes work that needs no
     code, or notes what it needs from him;
  5. report back: one Telegram message (telegram-alarm, the Hermes bot) that starts with the
     task number and says what a reply does. A task whose agent asked him something (a `⏳`
     note), or whose run ended without a word on the task, goes back to `todo` without the tag,
     so it is never retried blind: his reply (`reply` below) hands it back.

  task-agent              route new tasks, then work the next one (exits 0 when none is waiting)
  task-agent reply ID TEXT   his Telegram reply, passed on by Hermes: `merge`, `drop`, `mine`,
                             or an answer / change request (noted, and the task goes back to the agent).
                             His answer to a task's question on http://beacon/decisions comes the same
                             way, from answer-dispatcher (`reply --noted`: answered on the task).
  task-agent --dry-run    show the queue, the task it would take and the prompt
  task-agent sync-prs     only the PR check: a merged PR closes its task (own timer, any mode)
  task-agent pause|resume   stop / restart everything autonomous (routing and runs); the PR check,
                            the dead-run sweep, his replies and `--id` keep working
  task-agent --id N       work task N now (it must still be handed to the agent)

Scoped mode (TASK_AGENT_ONLY=project:tag[,...], 2026-11-21 task 1744): only tasks of that project carrying that
tag (and `claude`) are worked, nothing is routed, his page answers and PR states are left to him, and the global
pause doesn't apply (stop the timer to stop it). Today: clean-the-snow:discord, the Discord bug agent.
A repo with `.claude/agent/task-agent.json` brings its own run: a preflight before the claim (a failure leaves the
task queued), its own brief instead of ship-task, its own MCP servers and settings (hooks), env, a folder for proof
images that go to Telegram as photos, and the Telegram header (`from`). Its `checker` (task 1744, 2026-11-21): when
the run claims a fix that works (its last note `⏳ … Otestuj`) and changed the game, a FRESH run that never saw the
fixer's session verifies it from evidence only (the report, the fixer's shots, the real diff from the Studio gate's
log) in its own Play, and can reject it: a reject goes back to the fixer once, then the report says `neověřeno`.

Guardrails on a repo's own run (task 1744, 2026-11-21): at most 2 fix attempts per bug (then straight to him
with what was tried), a daily cost cap and his plan's quota, a circuit breaker (3 failed runs in a row stop it) and a
kill switch (`stop` / `start` on Telegram, or `task-agent stop|start`), the frozen "Hotovo když" every fix is judged
against (none = a question for him, no fix), the project's regression suite right before the fix and after it, a
Team Create lock before the checker, a scorecard with a weekly Czech summary (`task-agent scorecard`), and a report
he leaves unanswered is recalled after 2 days and closed after 7.

Uses the `tasks` command's client (next to this file) for the hub, TASKS_URL and TASKS_TOKEN.
Standard library only.
"""
